 But instead, I use the internet, and I'm going to send you a PDF.

109
00:06:48,840 --> 00:06:55,120
 And at this point, you might want to pause the video and grab this PDF.

110
00:06:55,120 --> 00:07:01,280
 Grab it, secretdecoder.pdf, drchuck.com/secret-decoder.pdf,

111
00:07:01,280 --> 00:07:02,360
 and download it.

112
00:07:02,360 --> 00:07:05,240
 And you might even want to print it out, because we're going to,

113
00:07:05,240 --> 00:07:11,760
 at this moment, do a code breaking exercise.

114
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:15,600
 And so let me tell you how to use this secret decoder ring.

115
00:07:15,600 --> 00:07:19,240
 So the top line here is the plain text.

116
00:07:19,240 --> 00:07:24,000
 And if you recall, Caesar Shift has a shift number.

117
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:28,280
 And so to encode, you go from a plain text.

118
00:07:28,280 --> 00:07:31,440
 Let's say I want to do Chuck.

119
00:07:31,440 --> 00:07:35,640
 I want to encode Chuck.

120
00:07:35,640 --> 00:07:38,580
 And I want to encode it with a shift of 2.

121
00:07:38,580 --> 00:07:41,400
 So a shift of 2 means we select this.

122
00:07:41,400 --> 00:07:46,680
 And we basically go C is our plain text.

123
00:07:46,680 --> 00:07:52,840
 And then down, we go E. So then we go E is our first letter.

124
00:07:52,840 --> 00:07:57,160
 And then H is our second letter.

125
00:07:57,160 --> 00:07:58,720
 So we go down.

126
00:07:58,720 --> 00:08:02,120
 And that means H becomes J. Yeah.

127
00:08:02,120 --> 00:08:08,760
 And so U becomes W. So now I'm doing my encryption.

128
00:08:08,760 --> 00:08:14,520
 So EHW is the encrypted ciphertext.

129
00:08:14,520 --> 00:08:18,560
 So let me clear that and write back down here.

130
00:08:18,560 --> 00:08:24,000
 Let me give me a different color, EHW.

131
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:26,720
 Now to decrypt, remember, you need to know the shift.

132
00:08:26,720 --> 00:08:29,440
 So we somehow communicated separately and securely

133
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:31,040
 what the shift was.

134
00:08:31,040 --> 00:08:34,120
 And so now we have received our ciphertext.

135
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:38,640
 We received our ciphertext.

136
00:08:38,640 --> 00:08:41,000
 And we need to decrypt it.

137
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:42,920
 And so we know that the shift is 2.

138
00:08:42,920 --> 00:08:46,640
 So we go to E. We go in the shift row.

139
00:08:46,640 --> 00:08:48,320
 And then we go up to the plain text row.

140
00:08:48,320 --> 00:08:52,000
 And that says the first one is C. Then we go to H.

141
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,960
 We go to the encoded text row.

142
00:08:54,960 --> 00:08:57,160
 And we go back up to the plain text row.

143
00:08:57,160 --> 00:09:01,960
 And so that's an H. Oh, wait, wait, wait.

144
00:09:01,960 --> 00:09:03,320
 It's not HW.

145
00:09:03,320 --> 00:09:04,080
 What am I thinking?

146
00:09:04,080 --> 00:09:06,480
 This should have been a J. I got this wrong.

147
00:09:06,480 --> 00:09:07,680
 Sorry about that.

148
00:09:07,680 --> 00:09:09,200
 So that's wrong.

149
00:09:09,200 --> 00:09:12,240
 Here's a J. Moves up to the H. Then the W.

150
00:09:12,240 --> 00:09:13,440
 Let's see if I got W right.

151
00:09:13,440 --> 00:09:13,940
 Yeah.

152
00:09:13,940 --> 00:09:16,920
 So W is my last ciphertext.

153
00:09:16,920 --> 00:09:21,240
 And it goes up to the U. Dot, dot, dot, dot, dot.

154
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:27,280
 So you see the pattern that our encoding is plain text down

155
00:09:27,280 --> 00:09:28,640
 to shift position.

156
00:09:28,640 --> 00:09:33,840
 And our decoding is shift position back up to plain text.

157
00:09:33,840 --> 00:09:36,080
 So this is our secret decoder ring.

158
00:09:36,080 --> 00:09:40,960
 So go grab it and download it so that you can participate

159
00:09:40,960 --> 00:09:43,920
 in the next exercise.

160
00:09:43,920 --> 00:09:50,260
 So now you are going to be cast in the role of Bletchley Park.

161
00:09:50,260 --> 00:09:50,960
 OK?

162
00:09:50,960 --> 00:09:51,680
 Ready?

163
00:09:51,680 --> 00:09:56,480
 So here is your first code-breaking exercise.

164
00:09:56,480 --> 00:09:58,080
 OK.

165
00:09:58,080 --> 00:10:00,600
 So here we go.

166
00:10:00,600 --> 00:10:02,160
 So you're Bletchley Park, right?

167
00:10:02,160 --> 00:10:05,960
 You just intercepted this ciphertext.

168
00:10:05,960 --> 00:10:12,600
 U-B-- oh, U-P-B-T-U. Whoa, it's encrypted.

169
00:10:12,600 --> 00:10:15,000
 It's clearly meaningless.

170
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:16,800
 So how are you going to decrypt it?

171
00:10:16,800 --> 00:10:21,240
 Well, the technique is take a look

172
00:10:21,240 --> 00:10:24,880
 and decrypt it with all the shift numbers, right?

173
00:10:24,880 --> 00:10:26,240
 You're going to do this by hand.

174
00:10:26,240 --> 00:10:28,360
 You're going to be a computer yourself.

175
00:10:28,360 --> 00:10:32,160
 You're going to do all the shift decrypting.

176
00:10:32,160 --> 00:10:34,440
 And just like in Bletchley Park, you

177
00:10:34,440 --> 00:10:38,220
 know you succeeded when the plain text makes sense, right?

178
00:10:38,220 --> 00:10:40,680
 When the plain text makes no sense,

179
00:10:40,680 --> 00:10:41,840
 then you haven't succeeded.

180
00:10:41,840 --> 00:10:46,480
 But at some point, the plain text makes sense.

181
00:10:46,480 --> 00:10:50,000
 So what you need to do is take your secret decoder ring,

182
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:53,440
 and you need to decrypt it with a shift of 1, a shift of 2,

183
00:10:53,440 --> 00:10:55,720
 a shift of 3, a shift of 4.

184
00:10:55,720 --> 00:10:57,640
 And if you have your family members around,

