 And it turns out the shift of 1 was the thing that we did.

252
00:14:05,760 --> 00:14:09,560
 So here is your second task.

253
00:14:09,560 --> 00:14:13,880
 This one's longer, and it's not a shift of 1.

254
00:14:13,880 --> 00:14:17,560
 And so this is a situation where you really

255
00:14:17,560 --> 00:14:20,080
 would have to get your whole family going on this,

256
00:14:20,080 --> 00:14:23,040
 where you've got to do 26 decryptions of this.

257
00:14:23,040 --> 00:14:26,320
 And it will make sense to you.

258
00:14:26,320 --> 00:14:29,480
 You decrypt it 26 times.

259
00:14:29,480 --> 00:14:31,960
 And so this one's going to be harder.

260
00:14:31,960 --> 00:14:34,080
 I guess you could just decrypt one word,

261
00:14:34,080 --> 00:14:37,320
 but it's just not a shift of 1.

262
00:14:37,320 --> 00:14:40,760
 But now we're going to do another trick.

263
00:14:40,760 --> 00:14:44,040
 So I don't want you to try all 26,

264
00:14:44,040 --> 00:14:46,200
 because there's a mistake in this.

265
00:14:46,200 --> 00:14:49,240
 There is a leakage of information

266
00:14:49,240 --> 00:14:51,960
 that makes it so that you can figure out

267
00:14:51,960 --> 00:14:55,480
 what the right decryption to try might be.

268
00:14:55,480 --> 00:14:57,440
 So this is English.

269
00:14:57,440 --> 00:15:00,000
 This is an English sentence.

270
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:04,280
 So stare at it for a while and find a more optimal way

271
00:15:04,280 --> 00:15:08,880
 to decrypt it than trying all 26 shift patterns.

272
00:15:08,880 --> 00:15:10,680
 So there's a way to optimize this,

273
00:15:10,680 --> 00:15:16,120
 a way to cleverly figure out what might be the best shift,

274
00:15:16,120 --> 00:15:20,520
 or how not to have to decrypt the entire message 26 times

275
00:15:20,520 --> 00:15:22,760
 to reduce the complexity.

276
00:15:22,760 --> 00:15:25,720
 And that's because we've leaked some information here that

277
00:15:25,720 --> 00:15:28,160
 should be pretty obvious to you.

278
00:15:28,160 --> 00:15:30,560
 So let me give you a moment to break this one.

279
00:15:30,560 --> 00:15:32,200
 It shouldn't take you too long, and you

280
00:15:32,200 --> 00:15:34,880
 shouldn't have to force your whole family to decrypt

281
00:15:34,880 --> 00:15:36,520
 this stuff.

282
00:15:36,520 --> 00:15:37,400
 So here we go.

283
00:15:37,400 --> 00:15:38,840
 Give you a chance to decrypt it.

284
00:15:38,840 --> 00:15:49,480
 Here's your last chance before the reveal.

285
00:15:49,480 --> 00:15:50,600
 You ready?

286
00:15:50,600 --> 00:15:51,080
 Here we go.

287
00:15:54,480 --> 00:15:59,840
 So here is the decrypted text.

288
00:15:59,840 --> 00:16:03,120
 The shift turns out to be 13.

289
00:16:03,120 --> 00:16:05,440
 It's a shift to 13.

290
00:16:05,440 --> 00:16:12,240
 And the weakness of this whole thing is this right here.

291
00:16:12,240 --> 00:16:16,440
 In the English language, what is a single character--

292
00:16:16,440 --> 00:16:18,480
 we're not encrypting the spaces, you'll notice,

293
00:16:18,480 --> 00:16:20,680
 because the spaces come across.

294
00:16:20,680 --> 00:16:25,760
 So what is the one single character word

295
00:16:25,760 --> 00:16:31,800
 that we have in the English language that's capitalized?

296
00:16:31,800 --> 00:16:37,160
 Well, that's usually I. I need a jet, money and a jet.

297
00:16:37,160 --> 00:16:42,880
 What is the one thing that we do in the English language that

298
00:16:42,880 --> 00:16:47,840
 is a single character word that is lowercase, typically,

299
00:16:47,840 --> 00:16:49,600
 unless at the beginning of the sentence?

300
00:16:49,600 --> 00:16:52,080
 And that is the letter A.

301
00:16:52,080 --> 00:16:55,000
 So basically, you didn't have to decrypt the whole message.

302
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:56,920
 You see some weird pattern.

303
00:16:56,920 --> 00:16:58,320
 And you go like this.

304
00:16:58,320 --> 00:17:00,280
 I just have to figure out.

305
00:17:00,280 --> 00:17:02,120
 And then you go look.

306
00:17:02,120 --> 00:17:05,880
 And you look in the row 13, the plain text.

307
00:17:05,880 --> 00:17:09,000
 And you go like, oh, where is-- you go look at the I,

308
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:10,760
 because you guessed to the plain text.

309
00:17:10,760 --> 00:17:13,120
 And then you just look down until you see the V.

310
00:17:13,120 --> 00:17:15,680
 And within seconds, literally, within seconds,

311
00:17:15,680 --> 00:17:17,800
 if you do it right, within seconds,

312
00:17:17,800 --> 00:17:19,560
 you know it's a shift to 13.

313
00:17:19,560 --> 00:17:24,120
 And then it's a trivial matter to convert it.

314
00:17:24,120 --> 00:17:26,280
 So you could figure out the shift code within seconds.

315
00:17:26,280 --> 00:17:29,720
 And this was how Letchley Park figured it out.

316
00:17:29,720 --> 00:17:34,040
 And this is why the known plain text was so important.

317
00:17:34,040 --> 00:17:36,200
 Because you'd only have to figure out like one letter

318
00:17:36,200 --> 00:17:37,960
 if you knew what the plain text was.

319
00:17:37,960 --> 00:17:42,000
 And often, they would know by length and certain other things,

320
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:45,800
 oh, I think we can guess what this plain text was

321
00:17:45,800 --> 00:17:48,080
 that this particular operator would send.

322
00:17:48,080 --> 00:17:50,320
 And this has to do with the leakage of information.

323
00:17:50,320 --> 00:17:53,000
 It's not the mathematical perfection or lack

324
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,200
 of perfection in the security key.

325
00:17:55,200 --> 00:17:56,520
 It's some other leak.

326
00:17:56,520 --> 00:17:58,720
 It's some other thing where they're going like,

327
00:17:58,720 --> 00:17:59,480
 oh, wait a sec.

328
00:17:59,480 --> 00:18:02,000
 I can take advantage of something.

329
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,880
 It was just equally encrypted as any other message.

330
00:18:04,880 --> 00:18:07,840
 But because I gave you this clue of an uppercase

331
00:18:07,840 --> 00:18:10,920
 single character word and a lowercase single character

332
00:18:10,920 --> 00:18:15,920
 word, lowercase single character word and uppercase single character

333
00:18:15,920 --> 00:18:18,200
 word, I greatly reduced the amount of effort

334
00:18:18,200 --> 00:18:22,000
 that you had to put in, OK?

335
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:25,760
 Now what's cool about this-- you can go to this website,

336
00:18:25,760 --> 00:18:30,440
 www.wrote13.com-- is that long ago, before Facebook

337
00:18:30,440 --> 00:18:32,320
 and before Twitter and before all these things,

338
00:18:32,320 --> 00:18:35,040
 we had these things called newsgroups.

339
00:18:35,040 --> 00:18:38,320
 And they were kind of this weird kind of collective email

340
00:18:38,320 --> 00:18:39,240
 list that we had.

341
00:18:39,240 --> 00:18:42,160
 And this was like in the '80s.

342
00:18:42,160 --> 00:18:44,360
 And it was even used in store and forward networks,

343
00:18:44,360 --> 00:18:46,880
 where it was kind of like Facebook and store and forward

344
00:18:46,880 --> 00:18:50,000
 networks, meaning that it might take four hours for you

345
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
 to see the status update.

346
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:55,280
 But we kind of would subscribe to these collective things.

347
00:18:55,280 --> 00:18:59,280
 And there was one that was basically the dirty jokes.

348
00:18:59,280 --> 00:19:02,800
 And the thing about dirty jokes was part

349
00:19:02,800 --> 00:19:05,160
 of what we were trying to do in this thing

350
00:19:05,160 --> 00:19:07,160
 was you weren't supposed to swear.

351
00:19:07,160 --> 00:19:12,600
 And there was software that would filter out swear words

352
00:19:12,600 --> 00:19:19,040
 from-- if you put a swear word in to a dirty joke,

353
00:19:19,040 --> 00:19:20,480
 it would not forward the message.

354
00:19:20,480 --> 00:19:24,180
 And so we had to have a way to encrypt messages that

355
00:19:24,180 --> 00:19:28,280
 included swear words so that we could tell dirty jokes

356
00:19:28,280 --> 00:19:30,240
 to each other for those who wanted to subscribe

357
00:19:30,240 --> 00:19:31,600
 to the dirty joke list.

358
00:19:31,600 --> 00:19:33,840
 And so they came up with this Rote 13.

359
00:19:33,840 --> 00:19:36,000
 So we came up with a simple Caesar cipher

360
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:37,760
 with a shift of 13.

361
00:19:37,760 --> 00:19:41,560
 13, beautifully, of course, is 26 divided by 2.

362
00:19:41,560 --> 00:19:43,160
 So it's a symmetric shift.

363
00:19:43,160 --> 00:19:46,600
 The shifting in by 13 is the same as shifting out.

364
00:19:46,600 --> 00:19:51,240
 So of all the Caesar ciphers, a shift of 13,

365
00:19:51,240 --> 00:19:52,560
 the encryption and the decryption

366
00:19:52,560 --> 00:19:55,080
 are exactly the same calculation.

367
00:19:55,080 --> 00:20:00,600
 And so we would type our dirty joke into Rote 13

368
00:20:00,600 --> 00:20:01,720
 and convert it to Rote 13.

369
00:20:01,720 --> 00:20:04,000
 And we would send it in Rote 13.

370
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:05,920
 And then we would-- if we wanted to decrypt it.

371
00:20:05,920 --> 00:20:08,440
 But what became funny after a while

372
00:20:08,440 --> 00:20:12,880
 was we were so used to reading Rote 13

373
00:20:12,880 --> 00:20:15,800
 that it almost became like a second language.

374
00:20:15,800 --> 00:20:17,320
 We could read second.

375
00:20:17,320 --> 00:20:20,400
 We could start reading the dirty jokes in Rote 13.

376
00:20:20,400 --> 00:20:23,160
 And we would laugh before we translated them out.

377
00:20:23,160 --> 00:20:27,320
 So Rote 13 is an interesting sort of historical thing.

378
00:20:27,320 --> 00:20:29,720
 And you go to Rote 13 and sort of encrypt

379
00:20:29,720 --> 00:20:31,000
 whatever you want to say.

380
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,040
 And I'll probably have some questions

381
00:20:33,040 --> 00:20:38,240
 to ask you where you will have to do some Rote 13 encryption.

382
00:20:38,240 --> 00:20:39,720
 And so that's the end of this lecture

383
00:20:39,720 --> 00:20:42,160
 where we talk about Caesar ciphers

384
00:20:42,160 --> 00:20:45,360
 and the various techniques and how Caesar ciphers work.

385
00:20:45,360 --> 00:20:50,080
 And so we'll be back and talk about cryptographic hashes.

